
The Real Battle in Year 8: Ordinary Income Taxes

Since penalties are off the table, your only enemy in Year 8 is your tax bill. Look at how the two strategies compare on taxes:

Option A: The Spreadsheet Plan (Convert $141,632)

• The Tax Hit: A $141,632 conversion counts entirely as ordinary taxable income.
• The Cost: This pushes you deep into the 12% or 22% tax bracket (depending on filing status), resulting in the $18,192 tax bill shown on your spreadsheet.
• The Friction: You are forcing taxable income onto yourself just to move money from your 401(k) to your Roth, while simultaneously pulling seasoned money out of your Roth to live on.

Option B: The Hybrid Plan (Pull $41k SEPP + $100k Roth)

• The Tax Hit: Your only taxable income for the year is the $41,000 SEPP. The $100,180 you pull from your seasoned Roth pool is 100% tax-free.
• The Cost: A taxable income of $41,000 keeps you in the lowest possible tax brackets. Your tax bill would drop from $18,192 down to roughly $2,000–$3,000 (depending on deductions).
• The Savings: You instantly save around $15,000+ in pure taxes in Year 8 alone.

